#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e84fac-02c3-5111-98b3-bc67fe1ec9ab" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c177ebb-74f2-5df6-ad9b-462f191cb199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238448} {:answer/id #uuid "4130a5d7-8c4d-5063-abeb-e26e9d5e5993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238622} {:answer/id #uuid "7b47653c-8ce4-54e4-a40b-0b94964d09dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239117} {:answer/id #uuid "e86b696c-29ba-5e81-84bb-95c894903bb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240100} {:answer/id #uuid "edae4073-9987-58fc-ad81-166cfa56cdaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edae4073-9987-58fc-ad81-166cfa56cdaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240148} :answer-field/id #uuid "7465bcae-e6d9-5d93-9ec3-ebbbe750de79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78c3ca4e-1ab2-52da-b32e-1ddeb67471e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-105498" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=8t-3$ and $g(t)=2t-1$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The distance between $f(t)=8t-3$ and $g(t)=2t-1$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖8t-3-(2t-1)‖ \\\\  & =‖6t-2‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨6t-2,6t-2⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6t-2,6t-2⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-24t+4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[12{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}+4t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(12-12+4)-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{4}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179731 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79e281dd-40c0-515a-a5d5-d063609ecf73" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals of Unity With Respect to X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105bce54-4026-504a-aebc-d3aeaf976b6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415624} {:answer/id #uuid "2e06e035-45fa-5e53-bfbc-65514f433cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415632} {:answer/id #uuid "c08436b7-7d2d-5354-b7f9-73e0c58fe847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415658} {:answer/id #uuid "dafff622-d36c-559c-aa25-3ffc0e5c93a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415662} {:answer/id #uuid "e3917919-8158-59b4-b944-e19f2eb5c017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e06e035-45fa-5e53-bfbc-65514f433cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415632} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c4667bc-8f59-530d-9f0d-9e00044a377f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4b4530c-a5d4-5bda-8a1d-c3d8365018f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-124306" :question/problem "![](images/05/05b76357203cf2d7ef38b4bfa8daa9e0ce21b248f150e1a2d516f1a4c4e5bef3.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along a quarter circle of radius $1,$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(1,1),$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to $x$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $x$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${x}_{1}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${x}_{2}=1$ and ${x}_{1}=0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1}=1-0=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00b575c8-36c5-5013-80f6-ee1498a6faf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493392} {:answer/id #uuid "01915aac-1c2e-585b-999e-bd689a77b856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493393} {:answer/id #uuid "63c50681-0310-54e1-a7ce-97fde423a4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493541} {:answer/id #uuid "99607e2e-69b1-5c13-81d9-aae71cd65b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493632} {:answer/id #uuid "d11c7d00-ca24-5099-854f-165cee581f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00b575c8-36c5-5013-80f6-ee1498a6faf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493392} :answer-field/id #uuid "131118e0-de48-5f9d-ada7-20da3689be40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dfb0578c-1343-53e2-a376-085feaa6ce26" :question/math-academy-id "q-140566" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157392 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38455e74-50c2-5581-b9e1-a8dd0c85f826" :knowledge-point/title "The Expected Value of the Sample Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c486c99c-b811-53ae-83c3-0c7aab1d91c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153833} {:answer/id #uuid "15d2e3fa-d01e-5c95-a8e2-403e72617751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153834} {:answer/id #uuid "2c7a1c7a-43c5-5fce-8c16-c057f1377ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153835} {:answer/id #uuid "65d544a3-d1a4-50d1-ad1f-b95f6f0c5d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153836} {:answer/id #uuid "6e87ea32-775f-5532-81a8-b88b06bf4edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e87ea32-775f-5532-81a8-b88b06bf4edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153837} :answer-field/id #uuid "85fbf89f-69ca-50e0-b2bc-314aac920c0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be802189-e841-547b-9c11-133fd34a8081" :question/math-academy-id "q-143187" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{50}$ is a sample of size $n=50$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =30$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=9,$ what is $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the population mean $\\mu =30,$ we have that $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=30.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172056 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b85acdfc-0505-5472-b5f3-53270bffa030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the y-Axis or z-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2477c6cb-c064-5421-afe0-a8e5def630d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493449} {:answer/id #uuid "39a9acae-d6a3-505a-9113-cf64e8d3f284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(z-1){sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493486} {:answer/id #uuid "7a9ef23b-b0fd-5e83-9f3b-d5c76fdedc47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493578} {:answer/id #uuid "b069f039-452f-5174-ba92-cb3de1a0b57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{z}-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493675} {:answer/id #uuid "e118469f-bcd0-5c50-aab2-4bee0d032ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(z-1)^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2477c6cb-c064-5421-afe0-a8e5def630d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493449} :answer-field/id #uuid "741e1c0e-e03a-5747-adb3-6e19f296eab1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7e0b385-be5e-5415-b9aa-b210f7036677" :question/math-academy-id "q-161046" :question/problem "$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨\\text{__________},\\,\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(z,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\sqrt{z-1}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [1,10].$ What is the first component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/04/04de4ea7dc617be1429c596812ce4f450c2cdaafd8d94d86f75e68a6743acba9.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(z)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(z)=\\sqrt{z-1}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,10].$ \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [1,10],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56559d07-36b9-5fba-b51e-1f8e5cba51e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "253\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389444} {:answer/id #uuid "c613f699-7516-5c10-a37c-3efc4c225fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "275\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389445} {:answer/id #uuid "796b1a5b-ed2a-5e7a-b39a-e70d3438bf86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "242\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389446} {:answer/id #uuid "9f89a0c0-4a4c-5eeb-833d-bfc360dbff90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "264\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389447} {:answer/id #uuid "66326f34-27cb-58c3-9945-4137e4710b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "280\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56559d07-36b9-5fba-b51e-1f8e5cba51e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "253\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389444} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b3b8e1e-c87a-5182-99ea-c5567dd2e35b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f249579b-a99f-5b51-b0c7-e7f72d9a4512" :question/math-academy-id "q-163678" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(3{t}^{2}+4)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=1\\,m/s$ when $t=0\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=3{t}^{2}+4,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n1=(0)^{3}+4(0)+C\\,⟹\\,C=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}+4t+1.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=(1)^{3}+4(1)+1=6\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=(2)^{3}+4(2)+1=17\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {17}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\  & =289-36 \\\\  & =253\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35a8694d-4fa1-54a6-a492-3025cb627fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0960efec-d285-5cdd-9e4c-60cdf590887c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493406} {:answer/id #uuid "75efba6a-e437-5328-8072-189d93667b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}35 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}-28 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493567} {:answer/id #uuid "a00c0b88-91a7-5321-9fb1-4c0eb83e8718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}-28 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493644} {:answer/id #uuid "baae230c-b658-5758-b99c-87c465155740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493696} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9a0dee-ec6c-54ab-9bd5-bc8eda85ad1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a00c0b88-91a7-5321-9fb1-4c0eb83e8718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}-28 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493644} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bc94f58-c888-5f48-8bc9-61a9a2954b43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2dd7bee1-71b3-5685-9b9f-df2041d5d283" :question/math-academy-id "q-79732" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=2 \\\\ {c}_{1}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n5\\cdot 6+{c}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-28.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+(-28)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}-28 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a981d24-47da-58b3-913a-1dbc73fb92fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448828} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b12f97-3b54-559e-bd17-0ff83f5d8e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2b12f97-3b54-559e-bd17-0ff83f5d8e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448829} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f982481-7fc9-54a0-8f4f-8749f44fb49d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449454} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1346c4e-4c1e-53ee-ac36-dc546d3f5769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448830} {:answer/id #uuid "f01f79a0-719a-58cd-b500-ac0068963a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1346c4e-4c1e-53ee-ac36-dc546d3f5769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448830} :answer-field/id #uuid "044d01a8-3f41-52ab-9651-438d24740dd7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449455} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5562d59d-df33-57ee-b141-b8df48965d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448832} {:answer/id #uuid "18f68743-873f-5f17-9aa2-9763f6a7656d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5562d59d-df33-57ee-b141-b8df48965d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448832} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ab07d31-23b4-5634-8396-1c695ff1a218" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45f8ab8c-12d7-5df5-b151-ee8250c362bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-96833" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- **II.** $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- **III.** $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is false. To factor out a constant multiple from the second argument, we must change it to its complex conjugate.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
